Yes, you successfully added port **8022**, which is the most important part! **However, you accidentally deleted the original port 80.** If you save it exactly like this, your Java application will work, but you will break the Hadoop Web UI for the rest of your team.
Before you save and close that editor, change your ports: section to look exactly like this so that **both** ports are open:
```yaml
  ports:
  - name: rpc
    port: 8022
    protocol: TCP
    targetPort: 8022
  - name: http
    port: 80
    protocol: TCP
    targetPort: 50072

```
Once you make it look like that, save and close the file.
### ⚠️ CRITICAL NEXT STEP ⚠️
Because this is a manual service (it has no selector:), saving this file is only **Part 1**. Kubernetes now has port 8022 open, but it doesn't know *where* to send that traffic.
As soon as you save and close that Service file, you **MUST** run the second command to update the Endpoints:
```bash
kubectl edit endpoints hdfs-namenode-1-svc -n cbops --kubeconfig h06vksuatcbopscls.conf

```
When the editor opens, find the ports: section and make sure it looks like this:
```yaml
    ports:
    - name: rpc
      port: 8022
      protocol: TCP
    - name: http
      port: 50072
      protocol: TCP

```
Save and close that second file. Once both are saved, your Thrift Server will instantly connect and your data will sync!
